 ### [Camera Pose Optimization via Depth and Silhouette](5667-camera-pose-optimization-via-depth-and-silhouette.md)
-This method optimizes an initial camera pose toward a ground truth pose by minimizing gradients computed from 2D depth and binary silhouette observations using a differentiable SDF renderer. It is distinct from sibling methods like [Instant-NSR-PL Forward Pass](2540-instant-nsr-pl-forward-pass.md) which may optimize shape parameters, by focusing specifically on pose recovery given a fixed shape. It differs from [Differentiable Silhouette Rendering](4322-differentiable-silhouette-rendering.md) by explicitly combining silhouette cross-entropy with a scale-invariant depth loss.
-
-### Reparameterization for Differentiable SDF Rendering
-A method using a reparameterization function $T$ for Signed Distance Functions (SDFs) that strictly satisfies the boundary requirement $\partial_\pi T(\omega_b, \pi) = \partial_\pi \omega_b$ [S5, unofficial]. It constructs a 3D vector field $\mathbf{V}$: $\mathbf{V}(x, \pi) := -\frac{\partial_x \phi(x, \pi_0)}{\phi(x, \pi) \|\partial_x \phi(x, \pi_0)\|_2}$, where $x \in \mathbb{R}^3$ is any point, and $\pi_0$ is the value of $\pi$ in the current scene (detached version) [S5, unofficial]. The evaluation distance function $t$ is constructed by reusing samples from sphere tracing: $t := \frac{\sum_{i=1}^N w^{(i)} t_i}{\sum_{i=1}^N w^{(i)}}$, where $t_i$ are intermediate distances and $w^{(i)}$ is a weighting function [S5, unofficial].
+This method optimizes an initial camera pose toward a ground truth pose by minimizing gradients computed from 2D depth and binary silhouette observations rendered via a differentiable SDF renderer. It is specifically designed for scenarios where the 3D shape is known (fixed) but the camera parameters are uncertain. Unlike [Differentiable Sphere Tracing](2316-differentiable-sphere-tracing.md) which focuses on general scene rendering, this method isolates pose estimation as the primary optimization target, using image-based errors to drive gradient descent. It sets itself apart from [Instant-NSR-PL Forward Pass](2540-instant-nsr-pl-forward-pass.md) by focusing on pose recovery from perturbed initial views rather than shape reconstruction from a single view, and from [Differentiable Silhouette Rendering](4322-differentiable-silhouette-rendering.md) by explicitly combining silhouette cross-entropy with scale-invariant depth loss.
+
+### [Reparameterization for Differentiable SDF Rendering](7868-reparameterization-for-differentiable-sdf-rendering.md)
+A differentiable rendering method for neural Signed Distance Functions (SDFs) that computes correct gradients with respect to geometric scene parameters (e.g., neural network weights) by reparameterizing the pixel integral to handle primary visibility discontinuities (silhouettes/occlusions) without requiring volumetric approximations or external geometric supervision like segmentation masks. It sets itself apart from prior SDF renderers (e.g., IDR [Yariv 2020]) by eliminating the need for per-view masks and volumetric approximations, and from mesh-based differentiable renderers by deriving a continuous warp function specifically for implicit SDF boundaries where direct sampling is challenging.
+
+### [Forward Rendering Pipeline with Closed-Form SG Integration](19274-forward-rendering-pipeline-with-closed-form-sg-integration.md)
+This method performs differentiable forward rendering of scenes defined by Signed Distance Functions (SDFs) and Spherical Gaussian (SG) approximations for appearance. It solves the rendering equation by representing both the environment lighting and the Bidirectional Reflectance Distribution Function (BRDF) as SGs, allowing the integral to be computed in closed form. It sets itself apart from sibling methods like [Instant-NSR-PL Forward Pass](2540-instant-nsr-pl-forward-pass.md) by explicitly modeling the specular component via SGs for relighting, rather than relying solely on neural radiance fields or implicit neural representations without explicit physics-based material parameters.
 
